WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.960
Beginning in 2013, women from all over
the world left their homes to join ISIS.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.860
Thousands of these women remain in Syria
and Iraq, where they continue to

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.150
fundraise and spread the group's
ideology. But women also played prominent

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:23.130
roles in combating ISIS. From Jordanian
fighter pilots to Kurdish infantry units,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.900
women were on the front lines. we
shouldn't be surprised. Women formed a

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:31.949
significant minority of fighters in some
of the world's longest-running conflicts,

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:36.780
including the civil wars in Colombia and
Sri Lanka. Women have served in many

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.550
roles in armed groups, maintaining camps,
and serving as medics and fighters. While

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:45.719
many women take up arms for the same
reasons as men, my research finds that

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:49.800
women are more likely to fight when
their homes, families, and safety are at

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.570
stake. An armed group's ideology also
matters: Women are more likely to join

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:59.160
groups that fight to protect and expand
their rights. The rise and fall of ISIS

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.449
demonstrates that whatever women's
motivations are, their participation is

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:06.700
now a fundamental feature of war.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.299
Does
women's growing participation in armed

00:01:09.299 --> 00:01:14.040
groups complicate the nature of war? Let
us know in our poll. Also, let us know

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.030
what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.330
sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

